insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 1, 'Petrophysics and correlation answer different questions. Which statement matches how the course positions them?', '["Petrophysics tells you how the wells relate, and correlation tells you what a single well contains","Both answer the same question, and correlation simply repeats it for more wells","Petrophysics tells you what a well contains, and correlation tells you how the wells relate","Correlation replaces petrophysics as soon as a field has more than one well"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course frames petrophysics as vertical and single-well work (what the rock in this well is) and correlation as lateral, multi-well work (how the wells relate to each other). Both are needed, and petrophysics comes first because you cannot correlate on character you have not yet understood.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 2, 'Which of the following is one of the four questions a correlation is described as answering?', '["Whether the structure is dipping between the wells","What the porosity is at each individual log sample","Which drilling fluid was used in each well","How radioactive the shale in the seal is"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The four questions are whether the reservoir continues between wells, whether it is thickening or thinning, whether the structure is dipping, and whether the wells penetrate the same units at all. Porosity per sample is a petrophysics result, not a correlation result.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 3, 'On a correlation panel, what does the horizontal axis represent?', '["True distance between the wells in metres","Well order, which is a layout choice made by the interpreter","Time elapsed since each well was drilled","Surface elevation of each wellhead"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A section is a display, not a map. Columns are usually spaced evenly regardless of real well spacing, so the horizontal axis carries ordering only. The vertical axis is real depth in metres and is quantitative.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 4, 'In the structural view of a section, how is each well hung?', '["Every well is shifted so that a chosen top lands on a common datum","Every well is stretched so that all wells share the same total depth","Every well is hung so that its shallowest top sits at zero","Every well is hung on its own true measured depth, with no shift applied"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The structural view is the honest, unmodified picture: no shifts, every top drawn at the depth it was measured. Dip is readable directly, but thickness changes are hard to judge because structural relief dominates the display.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 5, 'What does flattening on a chosen top do to the section?', '["It removes from the panel any well that lacks the chosen top","It rescales each well vertically until all the zone thicknesses match","It pins the chosen top to a datum depth by shifting each well vertically","It converts measured depth into true vertical depth"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Flattening applies one additive vertical shift per well so that the chosen surface lands on the datum and draws as a horizontal line. Wells that lack the datum surface cannot be shifted and are drawn unflattened and flagged rather than removed or guessed at.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 6, 'In the Ekene section, TOP_SAND is at 1565 m and BASE_SAND at 1601 m in Ekene-2. What is the SAND gross thickness in that well?', '["25 m","29 m","36 m","32 m"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Gross thickness is the difference between the two picks in the same well, so 1601 minus 1565 gives 36 m. That is the thickest SAND in the section, against 32 m in Ekene-1, 29 m in Ekene-3 and 25 m in Ekene-4.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 7, 'What is the structural relief on TOP_SAND across the four Ekene wells?', '["45 m","49 m","35 m","32 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Relief is the deepest pick minus the shallowest pick across the wells that have the surface. TOP_SAND runs from 1541 m in Ekene-3 to 1590 m in Ekene-4, which is 49 m. The 45 m figure belongs to BASE_SAND and 35 m to TOP_A.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 8, 'Why does the TOP_B correlation line reach only three of the four Ekene wells?', '["The TOP_B pick in Ekene-4 failed quality control and was removed","Ekene-4 reached total depth above TOP_B, so that surface was never drilled","TOP_B was spelled differently in Ekene-4 and no longer matches the other picks","Flattening removed TOP_B from the fourth well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-4 genuinely has no TOP_B because the well stopped above it. A missing top is a legitimate result, so the line simply does not reach that well rather than being extended to an invented depth.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 9, 'What depth range does the Ekene section cover in the structural view, and what is the span?', '["1495 to 1662 m, a span of 167 m","1500 to 1640 m, a span of 140 m","1541 to 1590 m, a span of 49 m","1495 to 1615 m, a span of 120 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shallowest pick in the section is TOP_A at 1495 m in Ekene-3 and the deepest is TOP_B at 1662 m in Ekene-2, so the structural window is 167 m tall. The 1541 to 1590 m range is TOP_SAND alone.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 10, 'Which Ekene well is both the deepest structurally and the one with the thinnest reservoir sand?', '["Ekene-1","Ekene-2","Ekene-3","Ekene-4"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-4 holds the deepest pick on TOP_A, TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND, and its SAND gross thickness of 25 m is the smallest in the section. Ekene-3 is the shallowest well on those same three surfaces.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 11, 'Which well has the thickest TOP_A to TOP_SAND interval, and what is it?', '["Ekene-1, 48 m","Ekene-2, 53 m","Ekene-3, 46 m","Ekene-4, 60 m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The TOP_A to TOP_SAND interval runs 48, 53, 46 and 60 m from Ekene-1 to Ekene-4, so the thickest is 60 m in Ekene-4. That is the same well that carries the thinnest SAND, at 25 m.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 12, 'Ekene-4 has TOP_SAND at 1590 m and BASE_SAND at 1615 m. The section is then flattened on TOP_SAND to a datum of 1500 m. What is the SAND gross thickness in Ekene-4 afterwards?', '["25 m, unchanged by the flattening","115 m, because the datum is added to the interval","It becomes negative, because the well shifts upward","It cannot be measured once a section has been flattened"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both picks in a well shift by the same amount, so any difference measured within that well survives untouched. The thickness stays 25 m. Only the displayed positions move, with BASE_SAND drawn at 1525 m while its true measured depth remains 1615 m.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 13, 'Why is a top hung on the wrong surface described as an expensive error?', '["The correlation software refuses to draw a section that contains one","It fails loudly at the next data import, forcing a rebuild of the project","It propagates silently into surface maps, thickness maps, volumes and well plans","It affects only the single well it was picked in, so the fix is local"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing breaks, because a bad pick is still a legal depth in a legal well. The error flows downstream into the surface map, the thickness map, booked volumes and planned well targets, and none of those products carries a warning that the pick beneath them was a guess.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 14, 'What is the log curve in each well column mainly there for?', '["It supplies the character used to argue that picks in different wells are the same surface","It sets the horizontal spacing between the well columns","It acts as the datum that the section is flattened on","It records the true vertical depth of the wellbore"]'::jsonb, 0, 'You correlate on curve shape, such as a distinctive spike or a sharp blocky base, far more than on absolute depth. The curves are the evidence and the tops are the interpretation drawn on top of that evidence.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-correlation-and-the-section', 15, 'TOP_B has 34 m of structural relief in the Ekene section. What caveat belongs with that figure?', '["It is the largest relief of any surface in the section","It was measured on the flattened section rather than the structural one","It includes the gross thickness of the SAND interval","It is measured across only three wells, so it is not directly comparable with the others"]'::jsonb, 3, 'TOP_B is absent in Ekene-4, so its relief of 34 m spans Ekene-3 at 1628 m to Ekene-2 at 1662 m and covers three wells only. Relief measured over a smaller set of wells is not comparable with the all-four-well figures for TOP_A, TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 1, 'Which three pieces of information does a formation top always carry?', '["A name, a well, and a measured depth","A name, a thickness, and a confidence score","A well, a lithology, and a true vertical depth","A depth, an interpreter, and a seismic horizon"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A top is a named depth in one well. Name, well and measured depth are the three things it always carries; thickness, lithology and confidence live elsewhere or nowhere.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 2, 'Why is a formation top described as an interpretation rather than a measurement?', '["Because measured depth is only known to the nearest 10 m","Because it records an interpreter''s belief about where a surface is penetrated, and two competent interpreters can pick it at different depths","Because logging tools are not calibrated below 1500 m","Because it is always derived from seismic rather than from logs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A gamma ray reading is reproduced by the tool. A top is an argument about geology recorded as a depth, so competent interpreters can defensibly differ by a metre or two.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 3, 'Two wells each carry a top named TOP_SAND. What makes them the same surface?', '["The shared name is sufficient proof on its own","They are the same surface if the two depths differ by less than 20 m","Someone correlated them and recorded that decision by giving them the same name","The section engine tests the log character and confirms the match"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The name asserts the correlation but does not prove it. A person had to decide the two picks are the same body of rock; the shared name is only how that decision is stored.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 4, 'In the data shape used in this course, what does each well hold?', '["A grid of correlated surfaces shared across all wells","A single top depth plus a list of log curves","A list of entries, each with a name and a measured depth in metres","A depth-ordered list of lithology codes with tops derived from them"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each well holds a list of name and md_m entries. That is exactly what the section engine reads when it looks up a top''s depth.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 5, 'Which log response most directly marks the entry into a clean sand as you go down the hole?', '["A sharp gamma ray drop from high values to low values","A sharp gamma ray rise from low values to high values","A steady resistivity value with no change","A gradual increase in bulk density over 50 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gamma ray responds mostly to clay content, so a sharp fall as you go deeper says you have left a shale and entered a clean sand. The reverse marks the sand ending.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 6, 'What are the three properties of a good marker bed?', '["Thick, porous, and hydrocarbon bearing","Distinctive, widespread, and easily recognised","Shallow, flat lying, and unfaulted","Subtle, local, and precisely dated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A marker must be unusual enough not to be confused with lookalikes, present across the correlation area, and quick to find even on a noisy log.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 7, 'Why does mixing pick conventions across wells damage a correlation?', '["It makes the tops table larger than the software can index","It changes the gamma ray values recorded at each top","It prevents the engine from gathering the distinct top names","It introduces depth differences of a metre or two that come from picking rather than from structure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Picking the top of the clean sand in one well and halfway down the shoulder in the next manufactures relief that is not real, or cancels relief that is.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 8, 'In Ekene-1 the clean sand runs from 1548 m to 1580 m, and in Ekene-3 it runs from 1541 m to 1570 m. What are the two gross sand thicknesses?', '["29 m in Ekene-1 and 32 m in Ekene-3","32 m in Ekene-1 and 29 m in Ekene-3","36 m in Ekene-1 and 25 m in Ekene-3","32 m in Ekene-1 and 36 m in Ekene-3"]'::jsonb, 1, '1580 minus 1548 is 32 m in Ekene-1, and 1570 minus 1541 is 29 m in Ekene-3.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 9, 'How many distinct top names does the Ekene section contain?', '["Three","Four","Five","Eight"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The set is deliberately clean: TOP_A, TOP_SAND, BASE_SAND and TOP_B, used identically in every well.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 10, 'An interpreter types SAND_TOP in one well and TOP_SAND in the other three. What does the section show?', '["One complete correlation line, because matching ignores word order","An error message blocking the section until the names are reconciled","Two half-populated lines, so the section appears to hold one more surface than the geology does","Nothing at all, because a name used by only one well is discarded"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Name matching is exact. The typo becomes a separate surface, and the resulting picture is a plausible looking alternative geology rather than an obvious failure.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 11, 'How does the section engine expose a typo in a top name almost immediately?', '["It gathers the distinct top names across the section and offers each as a correlatable surface, so the phantom name appears in that list","It compares each name against a fixed dictionary of standard surface names","It flags any top whose depth differs by more than 30 m from its neighbours","It refuses to draw any correlation line until every well carries every name"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine collects every distinct name present in any well and treats each as a candidate surface, so a misspelling shows up as an extra entry in the surface list straight away.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 12, 'A well returns a negative gross thickness between TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND. What does that mean?', '["The sand is overpressured at that location","The two tops have been swapped or mislabelled, which is a quality-control signal about the data","The interval was eroded, so the thickness is genuinely negative","The well was flattened on the wrong datum"]'::jsonb, 1, 'TOP_SAND is above BASE_SAND in every well, so the subtraction is always positive. A negative value is a message about the tops table, never about the geology.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 13, 'Which of these is NOT one of the four reasons given for a top being missing from a well?', '["The well reached total depth above the surface","The section was faulted out","The surface was eroded","The surface was picked but fell outside the logged interval of a neighbouring well"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The four reasons are total depth above the surface, faulted out, eroded, and not yet picked. What happens in a neighbouring well is not one of them.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 14, 'Ekene-4 has no TOP_B because the well terminated above it. What does the TOP_B correlation line do?', '["It interpolates a point for Ekene-4 from the neighbouring wells","It reaches 3 wells and simply does not extend to Ekene-4","It places Ekene-4 at zero depth so the line stays complete","It is suppressed entirely because one well is missing the top"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A well lacking a top is skipped when the line is built, so the line reaches 3 wells rather than 4. Missing is not zero and nothing is interpolated across the gap.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-formation-tops', 15, 'What does the engine do with a well that lacks the top chosen as the flattening datum?', '["It applies the average shift of the other wells so the display stays consistent","It removes the well from the section until a different datum is chosen","It draws the well at true depth and flags it, rather than silently hanging it on the datum","It assigns a shift of zero and reports the well as normally flattened"]'::jsonb, 2, 'There is no shift to compute without the datum top, so the well is drawn unflattened and flagged. A silent zero shift would make it look hung on the datum when it is not.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 1, 'In the engine''s terms, what shift does the structural view apply to each well?', '["A shift equal to the depth of the chosen datum surface in that well","A shift that lines up the shallowest surface across all wells","A shift of zero to every well, so displayed depth equals true depth","A shift equal to the average depth of all wells on the panel"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The structural mode is the special case where every well gets a shift of zero. Displayed depth equals true depth for all four wells at every point on the panel. Non-zero shifts are what flattening introduces.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 2, 'The shallowest pick in the Ekene dataset is TOP_A in Ekene-3 at 1495 m and the deepest is TOP_B in Ekene-2 at 1662 m. What is the displayed span of the structural section?', '["167 m","150 m","135 m","49 m"]'::jsonb, 0, '1662 minus 1495 is 167 m. The panel has to accommodate every pick, so its vertical extent is set by the two most extreme values in the whole dataset, not by any single surface.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 3, 'Why can you not read a dip angle off the Ekene correlation panel?', '["Because the depth axis is compressed to fit the 167 m span","Because the horizontal axis is well order, not map distance","Because TOP_B is missing in Ekene-4","Because measured depth is not the same as true vertical depth"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Wells are spaced evenly across the panel regardless of how far apart they sit on the map, so the section shows the sequence of the structure and not its true gradient. Converting sequence into dip needs well coordinates, which is a mapping task.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 4, 'Why can this course treat measured depth as standing in for true vertical depth?', '["Because the two are equal in every well once a datum is chosen","Because the difference between them is always smaller than the pick uncertainty","Because the correlation engine converts between them automatically","Because all four Ekene wells are treated as vertical, and in a vertical well the two are identical"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Measured depth is distance along the hole and true vertical depth is distance straight down. They are identical only in a vertical well. A deviated well needs a directional survey to convert, which is beyond this tier.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 5, 'How is the structural relief of a surface across a section defined?', '["The average depth of the surface across all wells on the panel","The depth of the surface in the deepest well on the panel","The deepest occurrence minus the shallowest occurrence, over the wells that carry it","The difference between the surface depth and the panel datum"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Relief is one subtraction, deepest minus shallowest, and it is taken only over wells that actually penetrate the surface. Wells that never reached it are excluded rather than counted as some default value.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 6, 'TOP_SAND sits at 1548 m in Ekene-1, 1565 m in Ekene-2, 1541 m in Ekene-3 and 1590 m in Ekene-4. What is its structural relief?', '["42 m","49 m","25 m","17 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The shallowest is 1541 m in Ekene-3 and the deepest is 1590 m in Ekene-4, so the relief is 1590 minus 1541, which is 49 m. This is one of the six numbers the Associate capstone grades.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 7, 'TOP_A sits at 1500 m in Ekene-1, 1512 m in Ekene-2, 1495 m in Ekene-3 and 1530 m in Ekene-4. What is its structural relief?', '["12 m","18 m","30 m","35 m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The shallowest is 1495 m in Ekene-3 and the deepest is 1530 m in Ekene-4, so the relief is 1530 minus 1495, which is 35 m. Using Ekene-1 rather than Ekene-3 as the shallow end gives 30 m, which is the common error.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 8, 'BASE_SAND sits at 1580 m in Ekene-1, 1601 m in Ekene-2, 1570 m in Ekene-3 and 1615 m in Ekene-4. What is its structural relief?', '["45 m","35 m","31 m","14 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shallowest is 1570 m in Ekene-3 and the deepest is 1615 m in Ekene-4, so the relief is 1615 minus 1570, which is 45 m.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 9, 'TOP_B is picked at 1640 m in Ekene-1, 1662 m in Ekene-2 and 1628 m in Ekene-3, and is absent in Ekene-4. How should its relief be reported?', '["34 m over four wells","45 m over three wells","34 m over three wells","22 m over three wells"]'::jsonb, 2, '1662 minus 1628 is 34 m, and only three wells carry the surface, so the well count must be quoted with the number. Ekene-4 is excluded rather than counted at some assumed depth.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 10, 'Why is the TOP_B relief of 34 m likely to understate the true relief of that surface across the field?', '["Because Ekene-4 is the deepest well on every surface it reaches, and it is the well missing from the TOP_B calculation","Because TOP_B is the deepest surface, and deeper surfaces always have less relief","Because three wells is too few for any relief calculation to be valid","Because TOP_B was picked on a different log curve from the other three surfaces"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene-4 supplies the deepest TOP_A, TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND picks. Had it reached TOP_B it would very likely have supplied the deepest TOP_B pick too, so the figure is systematically biased low by the absence of exactly the well that would have extended the range.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 11, 'Ranked from shallowest to deepest on TOP_SAND, in what order do the Ekene wells fall?', '["Ekene-1, Ekene-2, Ekene-3, Ekene-4","Ekene-4, Ekene-2, Ekene-1, Ekene-3","Ekene-3, Ekene-2, Ekene-1, Ekene-4","Ekene-3, Ekene-1, Ekene-2, Ekene-4"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The picks are 1541 m in Ekene-3, 1548 m in Ekene-1, 1565 m in Ekene-2 and 1590 m in Ekene-4. Ekene-3 is the structural high of the field and Ekene-4 is the low.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 12, 'TOP_SAND is at 1548 m in Ekene-1 and 1541 m in Ekene-3. By how much does the surface shallow from Ekene-1 to Ekene-3?', '["5 m","7 m","17 m","42 m"]'::jsonb, 1, '1548 minus 1541 is 7 m. That gentle 7 m rise contrasts with the 49 m fall from Ekene-3 into Ekene-4, which is why the section is a flank steepening away from a high rather than a uniformly tilted slab.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 13, 'All four Ekene surfaces rank the wells in the same order, Ekene-3 then Ekene-1 then Ekene-2 then Ekene-4. What does that agreement tell you?', '["That the four surfaces are the same geological surface picked under different names","That the interval thicknesses must be constant across the field","That the picks are mutually consistent and the structure they describe is real","That the wells were drilled in that order across the structure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each surface was picked independently on log character, so four independent picks agreeing on the same shape is strong evidence that both the picks and the structure are sound. Disagreement between surfaces would be a flag to go back to the logs.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 14, 'SAND gross thickness is 32 m in Ekene-1, 36 m in Ekene-2, 29 m in Ekene-3 and 25 m in Ekene-4. What is the spread between thickest and thinnest, and how does it compare with the TOP_SAND relief?', '["11 m, which is far smaller than the 49 m of TOP_SAND relief","7 m, which is about the same as the TOP_SAND relief","32 m, which is larger than the TOP_SAND relief","49 m, which is exactly the TOP_SAND relief"]'::jsonb, 0, '36 minus 25 is 11 m. TOP_SAND itself moves 49 m across the same four wells, and the panel is scaled to the full 167 m span, so the thickness story occupies about seven percent of the panel height and is drawn as a sliver.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-structural-section', 15, 'What is the explicit trade you make when you flatten a section?', '["You gain true depth and lose the ability to rank wells structurally","You gain pick accuracy and lose panel resolution","You gain both interval comparison and true depth, at the cost of longer computation","You gain interval comparison and lose true depth"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Flattening subtracts the structural component so thicknesses and thinning trends become directly readable, but every depth on a flattened panel is then a displayed depth rather than a real one. Quoting a flattened depth as a true depth is a standard beginner error.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 1, 'Which statement is the golden rule of flattening as stated in this module?', '["Flattening rewrites each well''s tops table so the datum top is stored at the datum depth.","Flattening stretches each well''s column so the intervals match the reference well.","Flattening changes the display, never the data.","Flattening changes the data, and the display follows automatically."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The stored measured depths are untouched. Flattening produces one shift per well and the engine adds that shift at drawing time only, so Ekene-1''s TOP_SAND remains 1548 m in the tops table whatever datum you choose.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 2, 'What is the engine''s formula for a well''s flattening shift?', '["shift = datum - md(top)","shift = md(top) - datum","shift = datum + md(top)","shift = md(top) / datum"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The datum comes first in the subtraction. Any depth in that well then displays at md + shift, and substituting the datum top itself gives md + (datum - md) = datum.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 3, 'The Ekene section is flattened on TOP_SAND at a 1500 m datum. Ekene-2''s TOP_SAND is picked at 1565 m. What is Ekene-2''s shift?', '["+65 m","-65 m","-90 m","+1565 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'shift = 1500 - 1565 = -65 m. The sign is negative because the datum is shallower than the pick, so the column slides up the display. Reversing the subtraction to 1565 - 1500 gives the sign error +65.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 4, 'Under the same capstone datum, Ekene-4 has a shift of -90 m and its BASE_SAND is picked at a true 1615 m. At what displayed depth does BASE_SAND draw?', '["1705 m","1615 m","1530 m","1525 m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'displayed = md + shift = 1615 + (-90) = 1525 m. Adding the shift instead of subtracting it gives the distractor 1705 m, and 1615 m is the true depth, which is unchanged by flattening.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 5, 'Ekene-1''s shift under the capstone datum is -48 m and its TOP_B is picked at a true 1640 m. At what displayed depth does TOP_B draw?', '["1592 m","1688 m","1640 m","1597 m"]'::jsonb, 0, '1640 + (-48) = 1592 m. The pair matters: 1640 m is where the surface is in the wellbore, 1592 m is only where it draws on this flattened panel. 1597 m is Ekene-2''s displayed TOP_B, not Ekene-1''s.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 6, 'Ekene-2''s gross sand is 36 m thick in the structural view. What is its gross sand thickness on the panel flattened on TOP_SAND at 1500 m?', '["1500 m, because the top lands on the datum","101 m, the shift added to the thickness","36 m, unchanged","It cannot be read from a flattened panel"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both ends take the same shift: the top displays at 1565 - 65 = 1500 and the base at 1601 - 65 = 1536, so 1536 - 1500 = 36 m. Thickness is a property of the well, not of the view.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 7, 'Why does flattening leave every interval thickness inside a well unchanged?', '["Because the engine recomputes thickness from the datum after shifting.","Because both ends of an interval receive the same shift, so the shift cancels in the subtraction.","Because thicknesses are stored separately from depths and are never shifted.","Because only the datum top is moved and the other tops stay where they were."]'::jsonb, 1, 'If a top moves from a to a + s and a base from b to b + s, the thickness (b + s) - (a + s) equals b - a. The whole column moves as one rigid block, so nothing inside it is stretched or re-ordered.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 8, 'Ekene-3''s TOP_SAND is picked at 1541 m. On the panel flattened on TOP_SAND at 1500 m, where does that pick display?', '["1500 m exactly","1541 m","1459 m","1582 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'By construction the datum top displays at exactly the datum in every well: md + (datum - md) = datum. Here the shift is 1500 - 1541 = -41 and 1541 - 41 = 1500. Checking that the datum picks form one flat line is the first sanity check on any flattened panel.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 9, 'A well in the section has no pick for the chosen datum top. What does the engine do with that well?', '["It interpolates the missing top from the neighbouring wells and flattens normally.","It removes the well from the section until the top is picked.","It applies the average shift of the other wells to keep the panel tidy.","It gives the well a null shift, draws it at true measured depth, and flags it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine never guesses. It returns a null shift, marks the well as lacking the datum top, and draws it unflattened with a flag, so nobody mistakes a true-depth column for a flattened one.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 10, 'On the Ekene section, which of the four tops makes a poor datum choice on the availability criterion?', '["TOP_A, because it is the shallowest surface","TOP_SAND, because it is the reservoir top","TOP_B, because Ekene-4 has no TOP_B pick","BASE_SAND, because it lies below the reservoir"]'::jsonb, 2, 'All four wells carry TOP_A, TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND, so any of those three flattens the whole section cleanly. Ekene-4 lacks TOP_B, so flattening on it leaves that well hung at true depth and flagged.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 11, 'The Professional tier''s growth view flattens on TOP_A at a 1450 m datum. Ekene-4''s TOP_A is picked at 1530 m. What is its shift?', '["-90 m","-80 m","+80 m","-45 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'shift = 1450 - 1530 = -80 m. The -90 m distractor is Ekene-4''s shift under the capstone TOP_SAND datum, and -45 m is Ekene-3''s shift under this TOP_A datum.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 12, 'Under the TOP_A datum at 1450 m, Ekene-2''s shift is -62 m and its TOP_SAND is picked at 1565 m. Where does TOP_SAND display?', '["1500 m","1627 m","1496 m","1503 m"]'::jsonb, 3, '1565 + (-62) = 1503 m. TOP_SAND is not flat under this datum, and it should not be: what hangs below the datum line is the TOP_A to sand interval, which is 53 m in Ekene-2. The 1500 m option is what TOP_SAND would display at only under the capstone datum.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 13, 'Flattening the Ekene tops on TOP_SAND at 1500 m changes the section''s displayed range. What is that flattened range and span?', '["1440 to 1597 m, a span of 157 m","1495 to 1662 m, a span of 167 m","1450 to 1600 m, a span of 150 m","1500 to 1597 m, a span of 97 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shallowest displayed top is Ekene-4''s TOP_A at 1530 - 90 = 1440 m and the deepest is Ekene-2''s TOP_B at 1662 - 65 = 1597 m. The 1495 to 1662 m option is the structural range, and 1450 to 1600 m is the range under the TOP_A datum.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 14, 'A well is given a negative flattening shift. What does that mean on the display?', '["The well''s tops have been recorded with the wrong sign convention.","The well''s intervals are compressed on the panel.","The whole column moves up the display, to shallower displayed depths.","The well is drawn below the datum line rather than above it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Depth increases downward, so adding a negative number gives a shallower displayed depth, which is higher on the panel. Ekene-4 has the largest negative shift, -90 m, because it was the deepest well at the sand and had furthest to travel to reach the datum.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-flattening-and-datums', 15, 'Someone points at the perfectly horizontal TOP_SAND line on the capstone panel and says the sand surface is structurally flat. What is wrong with that claim?', '["Nothing, since a flat line on the section means a flat surface in the ground.","The line is flat by construction, and the sand''s 49 m of relief is visible only in the structural view.","The line is flat because all four sand picks happen to share the same measured depth.","The claim is untestable, since relief cannot be measured from tops at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Flattening deliberately removed the datum surface''s relief, so the panel cannot be evidence about it. The picks run 1541 m in Ekene-3 to 1590 m in Ekene-4, which is 49 m of relief, and only the structural view shows it.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 1, 'In the Well Correlation course, what is a zone?', '["A named object stored independently of the tops, so it survives if a bounding top is deleted","The part of a well where log quality is good enough to pick a top","The interval between two correlated tops, named by the two surfaces that bound it","Any depth range an interpreter highlights on the section, whether or not tops bound it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A zone is defined entirely by its two bounding correlated tops. It has no separate existence, so removing one bounding top removes the zone.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 2, 'The engine''s zoneSpan function returns null in which situation?', '["When either of the two bounding tops is missing in that well","When the well has no flattening shift applied","When the base top is shallower than the top top","When the zone is thinner than 10 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule is two tops or nothing. If either bounding top is absent in a well, zoneSpan returns null and that well shows no zone fill and no thickness.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 3, 'A well has BASE_SAND entered at a shallower depth than TOP_SAND by mistake. What does the engine return for the SAND zone?', '["A negative thickness, which is drawn as an inverted fill on the section","Null, because the ordering is invalid","An error that halts the section from drawing","An ordinary span with top above base, so the mistake surfaces as a QC problem rather than a drawing artefact"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The span always comes back ordered with the smaller displayed depth in top and the larger in base. Reversed picks never produce a negative or inverted fill, so the error is caught by comparing thicknesses across wells.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 4, 'In the capstone flattened view, with TOP_SAND hung at a datum of 1500, what SAND span does Ekene-1 report?', '["Top 1548, base 1580","Top 1500, base 1532","Top 1500, base 1525","Top 1452, base 1500"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-1 carries a shift of -48, so TOP_SAND at 1548 displays at 1500 and BASE_SAND at 1580 displays at 1532.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 5, 'Ekene-3 has TOP_SAND at 1541 and BASE_SAND at 1570. What is its gross SAND thickness?', '["25 m","26 m","29 m","32 m"]'::jsonb, 2, '1570 minus 1541 is 29 m. This is one of the six numbers the capstone asks you to reproduce.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 6, 'Why is gross thickness the same in the structural view and in any flattened view?', '["Both ends of the zone carry the same per-well shift, so the shift cancels in the subtraction","The engine recomputes thickness from measured depth and ignores the displayed depths entirely","Flattening only moves the datum top and leaves the other tops at their true depths","Thickness is rounded to the nearest metre, which hides the difference the shift makes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Flattening adds one shift s to every depth in a well, so (base + s) minus (top + s) equals base minus top. The shift appears once with a plus and once with a minus and cancels.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 7, 'Which well has the thickest gross SAND on the Ekene section?', '["Ekene-4, at 25 m","Ekene-3, at 29 m","Ekene-1, at 32 m","Ekene-2, at 36 m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The four gross thicknesses are 32, 36, 29 and 25 m for wells 1 to 4. Ekene-2 is thickest at 1601 minus 1565, which is 36 m.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 8, 'The four Ekene SAND gross thicknesses are 32, 36, 29 and 25 m. What are the spread and the mean?', '["A spread of 7 m on a mean of about 32 m","A spread of 11 m on a mean of about 30.5 m","A spread of 11 m on a mean of about 32 m","A spread of 36 m on a mean of about 25 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The spread is 36 minus 25, which is 11 m. The mean is 122 divided by 4, which is 30.5 m, so the variation is about a third of the average thickness.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 9, 'What is the correct relationship between gross thickness and net pay?', '["They are the same quantity measured in different units","Net pay is the interval between the bounding tops and gross adds the shale beds on top of it","Gross thickness is the full interval between bounding tops, and net pay is what survives petrophysical cutoffs inside it","Gross thickness requires porosity and saturation cutoffs, while net pay needs only the two picks"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correlation gives gross thickness, which counts every metre between the two tops including shales and tight streaks. Net pay needs logs and cutoffs and is normally noticeably less. This course books gross only.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 10, 'What exactly does a correlation line connect?', '["The same top across the wells that carry it, in section order, at each well''s displayed depth","The same top across all wells, in order of increasing depth, at measured depth","Every top in one well, from shallowest to deepest","The two bounding tops of a zone within each well"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s correlationPolyline walks the wells in section order and returns the displayed depth of that top in each well that has it. Reordering the wells reshapes the line without changing any pick.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 11, 'In the capstone flattened view the TOP_SAND correlation line is dead flat at 1500 in every well. What does that tell you?', '["That TOP_SAND is a genuinely flat surface across the area","That the four wells were drilled from the same structural elevation","That the section has no structural relief worth discussing","Nothing at all, because the shifts were computed precisely to produce that flat line"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A flat datum line is a construction, never an observation. Structural statements must be made from the structural view, where TOP_SAND reads 1548, 1565, 1541 and 1590.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 12, 'In the flattened view BASE_SAND sits at 1532, 1536, 1529 and 1525 across wells 1 to 4. Why does it sit lowest under Ekene-2?', '["Because Ekene-2 is the structurally lowest well on TOP_SAND","Because Ekene-2 has the thickest sand, so its base falls furthest below the flat 1500 datum","Because Ekene-2 carries the largest flattening shift of the four wells","Because Ekene-2 is the deepest well drilled on the section"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With TOP_SAND pinned at 1500 everywhere, each flattened BASE_SAND depth minus 1500 is that well''s gross thickness. 1536 minus 1500 is 36 m, the thickest sand, so the line dips lowest there. It sits highest at 1525 under Ekene-4, the thinnest at 25 m.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 13, 'Ekene-4 has no TOP_B pick. How many wells does the TOP_B correlation line reach, and what does the engine do about the gap?', '["3 wells, because the engine simply skips a well that lacks the top","4 wells, because the engine draws through to the next well that has the top","4 wells, because the engine interpolates a position from the neighbouring wells","0 wells, because a line is only drawn when every well carries the top"]'::jsonb, 0, 'correlationPolyline skips wells where the lookup returns null, so the line reaches 3 wells. Drawing through would assert a pick nobody made, and interpolating would invent data.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 14, 'TOP_B is at 1640, 1662 and 1628 in Ekene-1, Ekene-2 and Ekene-3, and is absent in Ekene-4. How should its structural relief be reported?', '["As 34 m, with no qualifier, since relief does not depend on well count","As 10 m over 3 wells, taken from the flattened displayed depths","As 34 m over 3 wells, so a reader knows the statistic comes from a partial line","As 34 m over 4 wells, since the section contains four wells"]'::jsonb, 2, '1662 minus 1628 is 34 m, but it describes only the wells that carry the top. Quoting the well count alongside any statistic from a partial line stops a reader assuming Ekene-4 was covered.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-zones-and-correlation-lines', 15, 'Which reason for a missing top can be established from the Ekene section alone?', '["That the interval containing the top was faulted out","That the well is too shallow, since Ekene-4 reached total depth above where TOP_B is expected","That the top was eroded before the overlying section was deposited","That the top exists in the data but has simply not been picked yet"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-4''s deepest pick is BASE_SAND at 1615 and TOP_B is expected well below that, so the well ending too shallow explains the absence. Faulting, erosion and an unfinished pick list each require evidence from outside this section.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 1, 'In the five-step correlation workflow, what comes immediately after gathering the wells and their tops?', '["Choosing a datum and flattening the section","Looking at the structural view","Reading the zone thicknesses","Writing the report with the datum stated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The structural view comes second, before any flattening. It is the only view in which structural relief is visible, so the relief on each surface is noted there. Flattening is step three and removes exactly that signal.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 2, 'Why does the workflow insist on inspecting the structural view before flattening rather than after?', '["Flattening permanently alters the stored tops table, so the original depths are lost","Zone thicknesses can only be measured before a datum is applied","Flattening on a surface removes that surface''s relief from the display, so the relief has to be read beforehand","The correlation lines are not drawn until a datum has been chosen"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Flattening removes the datum surface''s relief by construction, which is the whole point of it. Reading the relief afterwards on the flattened panel returns zero. Flattening does not touch the stored data, and thicknesses and lines are available in either view.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 3, 'What drives the choice of datum in step three of the workflow?', '["The question being asked of the section","The shallowest top present in every well","The surface with the smallest structural relief","The depth at which the most wells have log coverage"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The datum is chosen for the question. Asking about the sand and what lies below it points to TOP_SAND; asking about the interval below TOP_A points to TOP_A. It is an interpretation decision, not a mechanical rule.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 4, 'Which figure from the workflow can be quoted in a report without naming the view it came from?', '["A top''s displayed depth","A well''s flattening shift","The position of a correlation line on the panel","A zone''s gross thickness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Thicknesses are view-independent, because both ends of an interval take the same shift and the difference cancels. Displayed depths and shifts belong to a particular view and must carry the datum with them.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 5, 'A project that should contain four surfaces shows five distinct top names, and the fifth name appears in only one well. What is the most likely cause?', '["A typo in the tops table, since exact-name matching turns a mistyped name into a new surface","A genuine surface that only one well was deep enough to reach","A well whose column is drawn at true depth because it lacks the datum top","Two picks entered in the wrong order in that well"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A phantom surface carrying a single well is what a typo looks like from the software''s side, because tops are matched by exact name. The fix is to correct the name, not to invent geology for it.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 6, 'A quality control run computes a zone thickness of -7 m in one well. What does that tell you?', '["The well was flattened on a datum it does not carry","The zone is genuinely absent in that well","The top and base picks in that well were entered swapped","The section was read in the structural view instead of the flattened one"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A negative thickness is impossible geology, not an unusual case. Base minus top must be positive in every well, so a negative result means the two picks are reversed in the tops table.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 7, 'Two correlation lines belonging to different surfaces cross each other between two wells. Why is this treated as a red flag?', '["Correlation lines are only drawn in the flattened view, so a crossing means the view is wrong","It means two surfaces swap order between those wells, which ordinary layered stratigraphy does not do","It shows that one of the wells is missing a top","It indicates the datum was set outside the displayed depth range"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A crossing means one surface is above the other in the left well and below it in the right well. The first suspect is a mis-entered pick in one of the two wells at either end of the crossing, not a real inversion.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 8, 'The Ekene sand thickness check is described as passing. What makes that spread believable rather than suspicious?', '["All four wells return the same thickness, so nothing needs explaining","The thicknesses are unchanged between the structural and flattened views","The thinnest well is more than half the thickness of the thickest","It is a modest variation about the mean, the kind ordinary depositional change produces"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The check asks whether the spread is a story you can tell. A gentle variation about the mean needs no special explanation, whereas an extreme outlier does. Thicknesses being view-independent is true but is a different check.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 9, 'On a flattened panel, one well''s datum top does not display at the datum depth. What is the likely reason?', '["That well lacks the datum top, so it has no shift and is drawn at true depth","That well''s shift was computed from the wrong surface","The datum depth was set shallower than the well''s shallowest pick","The zone thickness in that well is negative"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The datum sanity check exists to catch exactly this. Without the datum top there is no shift to compute, so the well sits at true depth beside wells that have been moved, and any depth read from it stands on a different footing.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 10, 'Why is every TOP_B statistic on the Ekene section reported as a three-well number?', '["Because TOP_B is the deepest surface and deep picks are less reliable","Because one well never reached TOP_B, so no pick exists there","Because the correlation line for TOP_B is drawn only in the structural view","Because TOP_B lies below the flattening datum"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-4 stopped above TOP_B, so the surface has no pick in that well and the correlation line stops short of it. Quoting a TOP_B figure as a four-well number claims data that does not exist.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 11, 'Which set describes the six quantities the Associate capstone grades?', '["Four flattening shifts, the displayed span of the section, and the count of wells carrying all four tops","One flattening shift, one zone thickness, two displayed depths, one structural relief, and one count of wells a correlation line reaches","Three zone thicknesses, two structural reliefs, and the datum depth","Two flattening shifts, two zone thicknesses, one displayed depth, and one predicted missing top"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The capstone grades Ekene-2''s shift, Ekene-3''s sand zone thickness, Ekene-4''s BASE_SAND displayed depth, Ekene-1''s TOP_B displayed depth, the structural relief on TOP_SAND, and how many wells the TOP_B line reaches. Predictions and growth readings belong to the tiers above.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 12, 'Of the six capstone answers, which one must be read in the structural view rather than the flattened one?', '["The zone thickness","A displayed depth","The count of wells a correlation line reaches","The structural relief on TOP_SAND"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The capstone view is flattened on TOP_SAND, which removes that surface''s relief exactly, so reading the relief there returns zero. Switch to the structural view for that one number. Displayed depths require the flattened view, and thicknesses and counts read the same in both.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 13, 'A well''s flattening shift is computed from its TOP_SAND pick. Which tops in that well does the shift apply to?', '["Every top in the well, including ones above and below the datum surface","Only TOP_SAND, since that is the surface the datum was set on","Only the tops that lie below the datum surface","Only the tops shared by all wells on the section"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shift belongs to the well, not to the surface it was computed from. The whole column slides rigidly by that one number, which is why a deeper top such as TOP_B takes the same shift as the sand top that produced it.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 14, 'What does the server require before the capstone unlocks?', '["Passing the final exam at 75 percent, with the capstone available at any point afterwards","Reading every lesson in all six modules, with quizzes and the exam optional","Reading every lesson and passing each module quiz at 75 percent for all six modules, then passing the final exam at 70 percent","Passing any four of the six module quizzes and then the final exam at 70 percent"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The deep path is enforced in order: all lessons read, each module quiz passed at 75 percent, all six modules cleared, then the final exam at 70 percent. Only then does the capstone open, and passing it grants the Associate certification.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-correlation-workflow', 15, 'Three failed attempts at the same module quiz trigger a 24 hour cooldown. What is the stated reason for it?', '["It limits how much load the grading engine handles in a day","It prevents a fourth attempt from being a guess rather than a reading, since three failures mean the material has not landed","It gives the server time to regrade the earlier attempts","It resets the pass mark for that quiz to a lower threshold"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cooldown is deliberate rather than punitive. Three failures in a row indicate the material has not been absorbed, and the day away makes the next attempt worth taking. The pass mark does not change.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'A correlation section shows Ekene-1, Ekene-2, Ekene-3 and Ekene-4 as four evenly spaced columns. A reviewer measures the screen gap between the Ekene-1 and Ekene-2 columns and asks how many metres apart the two wells are on the ground. What is the correct response?', '["Multiply the gap by the vertical depth scale, since both axes share one scale","The gap encodes the map distance between the wells, so it can be read directly","The horizontal axis carries well order only, so the gap says nothing about ground distance","The gap encodes distance only after the section has been flattened on a datum"]'::jsonb, 2, 'On a correlation section the horizontal axis is an ordering of wells, not a distance axis. Columns are usually spaced evenly for readability. Ground distance must come from the well coordinates, never from column spacing.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'In the Ekene fixture, Ekene-1, Ekene-2 and Ekene-3 each carry TOP_A, TOP_SAND, BASE_SAND and TOP_B, while Ekene-4 carries TOP_A, TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND only. How many individual formation top picks exist in the whole fixture?', '["16","15","12","14"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Three wells with four picks each gives 12, plus three picks in Ekene-4, for a total of 15. The fixture still contains four named surfaces across four wells; the count of picks is lower because Ekene-4 has no TOP_B.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'Which description matches the anatomy of a correlation section?', '["Each well is a vertical column, depth increases downward, tops are marks on those columns, and lines join tops that share a name","Each well is a horizontal row, depth increases to the right, and lines join tops that share a depth","Each column is a formation, each row is a well, and lines join wells drilled in the same year","Each column is a well, depth increases upward, and lines join the deepest pick in each well"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Wells are columns, the vertical axis is depth increasing downward, tops appear as marks on the well columns, and correlation lines connect same named tops from well to well.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'In the structural view of the Ekene fixture the shallowest pick is Ekene-3 TOP_A at 1495 m and the deepest is Ekene-2 TOP_B at 1662 m. What is the displayed depth span of that view?', '["150 m","157 m","162 m","167 m"]'::jsonb, 3, '1662 minus 1495 is 167 m. In the structural view every shift is zero, so the displayed span equals the true measured depth span of the picks.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Stated most precisely, what is the correlator''s job?', '["To measure the ground distance between wells so the section is drawn to scale","To decide which rock in one well corresponds to which rock in another, and to record those decisions as named tops","To convert every measured depth into true vertical depth before any picking begins","To choose the datum that produces the flattest looking section"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correlation is the act of asserting equivalence between rock intervals across wells, and the record of that assertion is a set of named tops. Everything else in the workflow supports or displays that decision.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'A section is redrawn with the wells in the order Ekene-4, Ekene-3, Ekene-2, Ekene-1 instead of Ekene-1 to Ekene-4. What changes?', '["The measured depths of the tops are mirrored about the section centre","The gross thickness of the SAND zone changes in each well","Only the left to right arrangement of the columns; every depth, thickness and pick is unchanged","The correlation lines break, because they can only be drawn left to right"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Column order is a display choice. Depths belong to the wells, not to the picture, so reordering changes nothing measurable. Lines still connect the same named tops, they simply slope differently on screen.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'Using the Ekene tops in measured depth (Ekene-1 TOP_A 1500 and TOP_B 1640, Ekene-2 TOP_A 1512 and TOP_B 1662, Ekene-3 TOP_A 1495 and TOP_B 1628, Ekene-4 TOP_A 1530 with no TOP_B), which pick is the shallowest and which is the deepest in the fixture?', '["Shallowest Ekene-3 TOP_A at 1495 m, deepest Ekene-2 TOP_B at 1662 m","Shallowest Ekene-1 TOP_A at 1500 m, deepest Ekene-1 TOP_B at 1640 m","Shallowest Ekene-4 TOP_A at 1530 m, deepest Ekene-4 BASE_SAND at 1615 m","Shallowest Ekene-3 TOP_A at 1495 m, deepest Ekene-3 TOP_B at 1628 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene-3 is the structurally highest well, so its TOP_A at 1495 m is the shallowest pick. Ekene-2 is the lowest well, so its TOP_B at 1662 m is the deepest. Those two values set the 167 m structural span.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'A formation top has been picked. What is the minimum set of information that makes it a usable record?', '["A depth and a log curve name","A name and a depth, with the well inferred from context","A name, a depth and the interpreter''s confidence score","A name, the well it belongs to, and a measured depth"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A top is an interpretation that carries a surface name, the well it was picked in, and the measured depth of the pick. Without all three the pick cannot be placed on a section or joined to picks in other wells.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'An interpreter types the Ekene-2 sand pick as TOP_SND instead of TOP_SAND. What happens on the section?', '["The pick is rejected, because only known surface names are accepted","A phantom surface appears with a single pick, and the real TOP_SAND line now joins only three wells","The pick is silently corrected to the nearest matching name","Nothing changes, because lines are drawn from depth order rather than from names"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correlation lines are built by matching names. A typo produces a new surface that owns exactly one pick, and it simultaneously removes that well from the real surface''s line. Name consistency is a QC item for this reason.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'Across the four Ekene wells, how many of the four named surfaces have a pick in every single well?', '["Four","Two","Three","One"]'::jsonb, 2, 'TOP_A, TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND are picked in all four wells, so three surfaces are complete. TOP_B is picked in three wells only, because Ekene-4 reached total depth above it.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'Ekene-4 has no TOP_B because the well reached total depth at 1615 m, above where TOP_B would lie. What is the defensible reading of that absence?', '["There is no pick because the well never sampled that depth, which is not evidence that the surface is absent there","TOP_B is absent at Ekene-4, so the surface has been eroded away at that location","TOP_B should be entered at the total depth value so the correlation line stays complete","TOP_B should be interpolated from the neighbouring wells and stored as a normal pick"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Missing data and geological absence are different claims. The well stopped short, so the surface was never tested. Inventing a pick at total depth or by interpolation would record a fabricated observation.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Suppose the Ekene-2 sand pick is renamed to Top_Sand while the other three wells keep TOP_SAND. How many wells does the TOP_SAND correlation line then connect?', '["Four","Two","One","Three"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Name matching is exact, so Top_Sand and TOP_SAND are treated as different surfaces. The TOP_SAND line drops to Ekene-1, Ekene-3 and Ekene-4, which is three wells, and Top_Sand becomes a phantom surface with one pick.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'What actually guides where a top is picked in a well?', '["The depth at which the same top was picked in the previous well, applied unchanged","An even spacing of surfaces down the logged interval","Recognisable log character and marker beds that can be traced from well to well","The midpoint between the shallowest and deepest logged depth"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Picks are anchored on repeatable log signatures, such as a distinctive shale marker or a sharp sand base, that a correlator can recognise in each well. Copying a neighbour''s depth ignores structure and assumes the answer.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'TOP_A is picked at 1500 m in Ekene-1, 1512 m in Ekene-2, 1495 m in Ekene-3 and 1530 m in Ekene-4. What is the structural relief on TOP_A across the four wells?', '["17 m","35 m","30 m","45 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Relief is the deepest pick minus the shallowest pick on that surface, so 1530 minus 1495 gives 35 m. Ekene-4 is the structurally lowest well on TOP_A and Ekene-3 the highest.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'TOP_SAND is picked at 1548, 1565, 1541 and 1590 m in Ekene-1 to Ekene-4. What is the structural relief on TOP_SAND?', '["49 m","42 m","25 m","0 m"]'::jsonb, 0, '1590 minus 1541 is 49 m. Relief must be read in the structural view, where all shifts are zero. In a section flattened on TOP_SAND the same surface displays flat, which would wrongly suggest zero relief.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'What defines the structural view of a section?', '["Every well is shifted so its shallowest top lines up with the others","Every well is shifted so the section span is minimised","The shift is zero in every well, so displayed depth equals true measured depth","Depths are converted to true vertical depth before display"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The structural view applies a shift of zero everywhere. What you see is measured depth, which makes it the only view where depths can be read off and quoted directly.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'TOP_B is picked at 1640 m in Ekene-1, 1662 m in Ekene-2 and 1628 m in Ekene-3, and is not picked in Ekene-4. What is the structural relief on TOP_B, and over how many wells is it measured?', '["22 m over three wells","34 m over four wells","102 m over three wells","34 m over three wells"]'::jsonb, 3, '1662 minus 1628 gives 34 m, and only Ekene-1, Ekene-2 and Ekene-3 contribute because Ekene-4 has no TOP_B pick. Reporting the relief over four wells overstates the well control behind the number.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'SAND gross thickness in the Ekene wells is 32, 36, 29 and 25 m, while relief on the bounding surfaces is 49 m at TOP_SAND and 45 m at BASE_SAND. What does this show about the structural view?', '["Thickness variation is larger than relief, so the structural view exaggerates thickness","The 11 m thickness spread is swamped by roughly 45 to 49 m of relief, so thickness change is hard to see in the structural view","Thickness and relief are equal, so either view reads the same","Relief is a display artefact, so only thickness is real in the structural view"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The thickest sand is 36 m and the thinnest is 25 m, an 11 m spread, against relief near 45 to 49 m. Structure dominates the picture, which is exactly what flattening removes so that thickness change becomes visible.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Relief across the four Ekene wells is 35 m on TOP_A, 49 m on TOP_SAND, 45 m on BASE_SAND and 34 m on TOP_B. Which surface carries the greatest structural relief?', '["TOP_A","BASE_SAND","TOP_SAND","TOP_B"]'::jsonb, 2, 'TOP_SAND at 49 m is the largest of the four values. Note that the TOP_B figure of 34 m rests on three wells only, so it is not directly comparable well for well with the others.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'A drilling engineer asks which depth to expect for BASE_SAND at a location near Ekene-2. Which view should the answer be taken from?', '["The structural view, because its displayed depths are true measured depths","The view flattened on TOP_SAND, because it removes structural noise","The view flattened on TOP_A, because TOP_A is the shallowest surface","Any view, because flattening does not change the numbers on the depth axis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only the structural view displays true measured depth, since all shifts are zero there. Any flattened view shows depths that have been moved by a per well shift and must never be quoted as drilling depths.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'In the structural view, Ekene-3 TOP_A displays at 1495 m and Ekene-2 TOP_A displays at 1512 m. How far below Ekene-3 TOP_A does Ekene-2 TOP_A appear?', '["35 m","12 m","49 m","17 m"]'::jsonb, 3, '1512 minus 1495 is 17 m. Because the structural view applies zero shift everywhere, on screen separation between two picks equals their true measured depth difference.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Ekene-2 has TOP_SAND at a measured depth of 1565 m. The section is flattened on TOP_SAND with a datum of 1500 m. What shift is applied to Ekene-2?', '["+65 m","-65 m","-1500 m","+1565 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The shift is datum minus the measured depth of the flattening top, so 1500 minus 1565 gives -65 m. The sign is negative because the pick sits below the datum and must be lifted to reach it.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'Which pair of formulas governs flattening?', '["shift = datum - md(top), and displayed = md + shift","shift = md(top) - datum, and displayed = md + shift","shift = datum - md(top), and displayed = md - shift","shift = datum + md(top), and displayed = md - shift"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shift for a well is the datum minus the measured depth of the flattening top in that well, and every pick in that well displays at its measured depth plus that one shift. Swapping either sign moves the wells the wrong way.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'Ekene-4 has TOP_SAND at 1590 m and BASE_SAND at 1615 m. The section is flattened on TOP_SAND at a datum of 1500 m. What shift does Ekene-4 receive, and where does its BASE_SAND display?', '["Shift +90 m, BASE_SAND displays at 1705 m","Shift -90 m, BASE_SAND displays at 1615 m","Shift -90 m, BASE_SAND displays at 1525 m","Shift -115 m, BASE_SAND displays at 1500 m"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The shift is 1500 minus 1590, which is -90 m. BASE_SAND then displays at 1615 plus -90, which is 1525 m. Its true measured depth is still 1615 m; only the display has moved.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'In the Professional preview the section is flattened on TOP_A at a datum of 1450 m. Ekene-4 has TOP_A at 1530 m and TOP_SAND at 1590 m. Where does Ekene-4 TOP_SAND display?', '["1590 m","1500 m","1450 m","1510 m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The shift is 1450 minus 1530, which is -80 m. TOP_SAND then displays at 1590 plus -80, which is 1510 m. It is the deepest displayed TOP_SAND in that view, reflecting the 60 m TOP_A to TOP_SAND interval in Ekene-4.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'A section flattened on TOP_SAND at a datum of 1500 m shows Ekene-1 TOP_B at 1592 m. A colleague asks what depth to log for Ekene-1 TOP_B in the well database. What is correct?', '["1592 m, since that is what the current section shows","1640 m, because the database stores true measured depth and 1592 m is a displayed value","1546 m, the average of the displayed and datum values","Either value, provided the datum is noted alongside it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-1 carries a shift of -48 m in that view, so a true depth of 1640 m displays at 1592 m. Stored picks are always true measured depths. Displayed depths exist only inside one view and change with the datum.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'When does flattening most seriously mislead an interpreter?', '["When the datum value is not a round number, which distorts the shifts","When the flattening surface is present in every well, because the section then looks too regular","When the flattening surface was not close to horizontal at the time of deposition, so the flattened picture is not a restored geometry","When more than three wells are shown, because the shifts accumulate across the section"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Flattening asserts that the chosen surface was level when it formed. If that assumption fails, the apparent thickening and thinning around it is an artefact of the datum choice, not a depositional result. Shifts do not accumulate and the datum value itself is arbitrary.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'The structural view of the Ekene fixture spans 167 m, from 1495 to 1662 m. The capstone view flattened on TOP_SAND at 1500 m spans 157 m, from 1440 to 1597 m. What is the change in span, and why does it happen?', '["It narrows by 10 m, because flattening removes part of the structural relief from the display","It narrows by 10 m, because flattening deletes the Ekene-4 TOP_B pick","It widens by 10 m, because the shifts push wells further apart vertically","It is unchanged, because flattening never alters the displayed span"]'::jsonb, 0, '167 minus 157 is 10 m. Aligning TOP_SAND removes the relief on that surface from the display, so the remaining spread of picks is tighter. Ekene-4 never had a TOP_B pick, in either view.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'Ekene-3 has TOP_SAND at 1541 m and BASE_SAND at 1570 m. What is the gross thickness of the SAND zone in Ekene-3?', '["32 m","36 m","29 m","25 m"]'::jsonb, 2, '1570 minus 1541 is 29 m. A zone spans between its two bounding tops in a single well, so its gross thickness is simply the difference of those two measured depths.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Why is gross zone thickness described as view independent?', '["Both bounding tops in a well receive the same shift, so their difference is unchanged in any view","Thickness is stored separately from the tops and is never recomputed","Thickness is measured in true vertical depth, which flattening does not touch","Flattening rescales the depth axis so thickness is restored to its structural value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A flattening shift is one number per well applied to every pick in that well. Subtracting two shifted depths cancels the shift, so thickness is identical in the structural view and in any flattened view.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'In the capstone view flattened on TOP_SAND at 1500 m, Ekene-2 shows TOP_SAND at 1500 m and BASE_SAND at 1536 m. What is the SAND gross thickness in Ekene-2?', '["65 m","36 m","32 m","25 m"]'::jsonb, 1, '1536 minus 1500 is 36 m, which matches 1601 minus 1565 in true measured depth. The shift of -65 m applies to both picks and cancels in the subtraction, so flattening cannot change thickness.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'TOP_B is picked in Ekene-1, Ekene-2 and Ekene-3 but not in Ekene-4, and the wells are displayed in order Ekene-1 to Ekene-4. How many wells does the TOP_B correlation line reach, and how many segments does it draw?', '["Four wells and three segments","Three wells and three segments","Two wells and one segment","Three wells and two segments"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The line joins the three wells that have the pick, which needs two segments, and then stops short at Ekene-4 rather than continuing to a well with no pick. Counting four wells is the common off by one error here.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'What is a correlation line, strictly speaking?', '["A measured surface trace produced by interpolating between the wells","A rendering of seismic reflection continuity between the well locations","A boundary that constrains where future picks in those wells may be placed","A drawn assertion that the picks it joins represent the same surface"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The line adds no data. It states the interpreter''s claim that the joined picks are the same geological surface, and it is only as good as the picks and the reasoning behind them.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'The TOP_A to TOP_SAND interval measures 48 m in Ekene-1, 53 m in Ekene-2, 46 m in Ekene-3 and 60 m in Ekene-4. Which well has the thickest interval, and by how much does it exceed the thinnest?', '["Ekene-2, by 7 m","Ekene-4, by 14 m","Ekene-4, by 12 m","Ekene-3, by 14 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-4 at 60 m is the thickest and Ekene-3 at 46 m is the thinnest, so the spread is 14 m. This same 14 m spread is what the Professional view flattened on TOP_A makes visible as the scatter of displayed TOP_SAND depths.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Ekene-3 has BASE_SAND at 1570 m and TOP_B at 1628 m. What is the gross thickness of the zone between them, and in how many of the four wells can this zone be measured at all?', '["58 m, in three wells","58 m, in four wells","61 m, in three wells","45 m, in three wells"]'::jsonb, 0, '1628 minus 1570 gives 58 m. The zone needs both bounding tops, so it exists in Ekene-1 at 60 m, Ekene-2 at 61 m and Ekene-3 at 58 m, but not in Ekene-4, which has no TOP_B.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Which sequence matches the correlation workflow taught in this course?', '["Flatten on a datum, then pick tops onto the flattened display, then read structure","Define zones first, then pick the tops that bound them, then load the wells","Pick tops, define zones, then load the wells that the zones require","Load the wells, pick and name the tops, build the structural section, flatten on a datum, define zones, then QC the result"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Wells and their logs come first, then picks, then the structural section that shows true depth, then flattening as a way of looking at the same picks, then zones built from bounding tops, with QC over the whole result. Picking onto a flattened display would bake a datum assumption into the data.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Which set of checks belongs in the QC step of the workflow?', '["Surface names spelled consistently, tops in sensible depth order within each well, and every missing pick explained","Every well carrying the same number of picks, and every zone the same thickness","Every correlation line spanning all wells, and every shift being negative","The section span minimised, and the datum set to a round number"]'::jsonb, 0, 'QC targets things that can be wrong in the data: typos that split a surface, picks out of stratigraphic order, and absences that need a stated reason such as Ekene-4 reaching total depth above TOP_B. Forcing every line to span all wells would mean inventing picks.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'Which six quantities does the DC6 capstone grade?', '["Four structural reliefs plus the structural span and the flattened span","The four SAND thicknesses plus the datum and the section well count","Ekene-2 flattening shift, Ekene-3 SAND thickness, Ekene-4 BASE_SAND displayed depth, TOP_SAND structural relief, the number of wells the TOP_B line reaches, and Ekene-1 TOP_B displayed depth","Ekene-2 flattening shift, Ekene-3 SAND thickness, Ekene-4 BASE_SAND true depth, TOP_SAND flattened relief, the total pick count, and the datum value"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The capstone grades one shift, one thickness, two displayed depths, one relief and one well count. Together they test shift arithmetic, view independence of thickness, displayed versus true depth, reading relief in the structural view, and partial correlation lines.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'Ekene-1 has TOP_SAND at 1548 m and TOP_B at 1640 m. In the capstone view, flattened on TOP_SAND at a datum of 1500 m, where does Ekene-1 TOP_B display?', '["1688 m","1592 m","1640 m","1548 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The shift is 1500 minus 1548, which is -48 m, and 1640 plus -48 gives 1592 m. Adding the shift with the wrong sign gives 1688 m, and leaving the pick at 1640 m ignores the shift entirely.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Which statement matches the assessment gates for this course?', '["Module quizzes need 70 percent, the final exam needs 75 percent, and the capstone may be taken at any time","Module quizzes need 75 percent with no retry limit, and the final exam is optional once the capstone is passed","Module quizzes and the final exam both need 70 percent, with a 24 hour cooldown after a single fail","Module quizzes need 75 percent with a 24 hour cooldown after three fails, the final exam needs 70 percent, and the capstone comes last"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The module gate is 75 percent, three failed attempts trigger a 24 hour cooldown, the final exam gate is 70 percent, and the capstone is the last item in the course.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'One capstone item asks for the structural relief on TOP_SAND while the working section is flattened on TOP_SAND at 1500 m. What is the correct answer and the correct method?', '["0 m, because every TOP_SAND pick displays at the datum in the working view","90 m, the largest single shift applied in the flattened view","49 m, read from the true measured depths in the structural view","157 m, the displayed span of the flattened view"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Relief is a property of the surface in true depth, so it is read in the structural view: 1590 minus 1541 gives 49 m. In the flattened view TOP_SAND is level by construction, which is why reading relief there returns a meaningless zero.'),
  ('wellcorrelation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'What do the Professional and Expert previews at the end of the course indicate?', '["That the same fixture and skills extend into deeper work, such as choosing among competing datums and correlating with fewer or lower quality picks","That the beginner tops must be repicked before either tier can be started","That the Professional tier replaces measured depth with true vertical depth and abandons formation tops","That the capstone score is carried forward as the starting score for the next tier"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The previews show where the beginner material leads. The section, the tops and the flattening arithmetic stay the same, while the interpretive questions get harder, including which datum to trust and how to correlate where control is sparse.');
